00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:22.272
<v NARRATOR>Bramble met Flourish on a bright windy evening, in the middle of a story that wouldn't quite land.

4
00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:47.843
<v NARRATOR>A young creature was telling a tale about a giant mountain. "It was a really, really big mountain. Like, super big. The biggest." But she stood perfectly still, paws flat at her sides — and though she said "big" three times, nobody could feel the bigness. The mountain stayed small in everyone's mind.

5
00:00:47.843 --> 00:00:52.254
<v FLOURISH>She told us it was big. But she didn't SHOW us.

6
00:00:52.254 --> 00:01:09.825
<v NARRATOR>And then the tall crane spread her great wings up and out, slow and wide, filling the whole sky above the fire — and suddenly Bramble could see it. A mountain. An enormous one.

7
00:01:09.825 --> 00:01:14.316
<v BRAMBLE>You drew it in the air. Just with your wings.

8
00:01:14.316 --> 00:01:38.007
<v FLOURISH>My name is Flourish. When I tell a story, my body helps. If a thing is huge, I make it huge. If a thing is tiny — I draw my wing-tips together until they nearly touch, and lean in small. The words say it. The body shows it. Together, the listener can really see.

9
00:01:38.007 --> 00:01:51.858
<v NARRATOR>To prove it, she told a quick tale about a tiny seed that grew into a towering tree. She started crouched low, wings tucked into a ball, voice small.

10
00:01:51.858 --> 00:02:08.269
<v FLOURISH>And the seed pushed up, and up, past the ferns, past the branches, higher and higher, until it was a great tree stretched all the way toward the stars.

11
00:02:08.269 --> 00:02:21.160
<v NARRATOR>The whole circle's eyes went up with her. They watched the tree grow right there in the firelight, made of nothing but a crane and the air.

12
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:33.851
<v BRAMBLE>The kids in my circle stand stiff as posts. Their giants and mountains stay small because their bodies stay still. Would you teach them to use their hands?

13
00:02:33.851 --> 00:02:49.022
<v FLOURISH>I would love to. But one gentle rule: your hands should MATCH your story, not just wave about. A wild flap that means nothing only distracts. Small and true beats big and busy.

14
00:02:49.022 --> 00:03:01.513
<v NARRATOR>When body and words say the same thing at the same time, Bramble noticed, there was a settled, sure feeling to it — like the story stood on two legs at last.

15
00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:07.564
<v FLOURISH>I can't help moving when I tell a story. The story gets into my wings.

16
00:03:07.564 --> 00:03:19.455
<v NARRATOR>And so the circle learned to let their tales into their hands — and their mountains, at last, rose up huge in the firelight.

